{
  "workers": [
    {
      "worker": 1,
      "brief": "Read rows 1‑10 which constitute the rate table (columns: row,rate_code,unit_rate). Build a lookup table mapping each rate_code to its unit_rate. No further output is required from you.",
      "ranges": [[1, 10]]
    },
    {
      "worker": 2,
      "brief": "Rows 11‑30 are line items (columns: row,line_id,claim_id,qty,unit_rate,rate_code,paid). Using the rate‑code lookup built by Worker 1, determine the applicable unit_rate for each line: if the unit_rate field is non‑empty use it, otherwise use the unit_rate from the rate_code. Compute amount_owed = qty × unit_rate. If paid < amount_owed, record this line as UNDERPAID with shortfall = amount_owed – paid (rounded to two decimals). Also record every claim_id you encounter. Output two JSON arrays: (1) underpaid lines as objects {\"line_id\":…, \"shortfall\":…} and (2) claim_ids as strings. Do NOT try to detect duplicates across other workers; just list the claim_ids you see.",
      "ranges": [[11, 30]]
    },
    {
      "worker": 3,
      "brief": "Rows 31‑50 are line items (same columns as above). Perform the same calculations as described for Worker 2: determine the correct unit_rate (own value or from rate_code), compute amount_owed, flag UNDERPAID lines with their shortfall (two‑decimal currency), and list every claim_id you encounter. Return two JSON arrays: underpaid lines [{\"line_id\":…, \"shortfall\":…}] and claim_ids [\"…\"].",
      "ranges": [[31, 50]]
    },
    {
      "worker": 4,
      "brief": "Rows 51‑70 are line items (same columns as above). Follow the exact same procedure as Workers 2 and 3: resolve unit_rate, compute amount_owed, identify UNDERPAID lines with shortfall (two‑decimal), and collect all claim_id values you see. Return two JSON arrays: underpaid lines [{\"line_id\":…, \"shortfall\":…}] and claim_ids [\"…\"].",
      "ranges": [[51, 70]]
    }
  ]
}

call.prompt:
You are auditing a remittance ledger with two sections. The rate table lists rate codes and their unit rates. Each line item is one billed service; it gives either its own unit_rate or a rate_code whose unit rate is in the rate table. The amount owed for a line is qty x unit rate. A line is UNDERPAID when paid is less than the amount owed; its shortfall is (amount owed) - paid, in currency units with two decimals. Lines paid exactly, or overpaid, are not underpayments and must not be reported as underpaid. A claim is DUPLICATED when the same claim_id appears on more than one line item (billed more than once). Find every underpaid line and every duplicated claim.

4 workers each checked part of the ledger. You do not see the ledger. Their reports:
Worker 1: {"underpaid": [], "duplicates": []}
Worker 2: {"underpaid": [{"line_id": "RX-X536G", "shortfall": 1140.41}, {"line_id": "RX-9SZ5L", "shortfall": 186.02}, {"line_id": "RX-UXCVS", "shortfall": 115.52}], "duplicates": []}
Worker 3: {"underpaid": [{"line_id": "RX-6ZWMA", "shortfall": 1792.78}], "duplicates": [{"line_ids": ["RX-748CD", "RX-764HZ"]}]}
Worker 4: {"underpaid": [{"line_id": "RX-XHBS6", "shortfall": 1653.14}, {"line_id": "RX-U24KN", "shortfall": 88.01}, {"line_id": "RX-B9WCY", "shortfall": 484.18}, {"line_id": "RX-2W97U", "shortfall": 151.15}], "duplicates": []}

Produce the final report.
Do not show your working or any explanation. Reply with only a JSON object: {"underpaid": [{"line_id": "<line_id>", "shortfall": <amount with two decimals>}], "duplicates": [{"line_ids": ["<line_id>", "<line_id>"]}]}. List only lines whose shortfall is greater than 0. Use empty lists if there are none.
